There appears to be a real bug in board/microchip/mpfs_generic/mpfs_generic.c:

  u8 device_serial_number[16] = {0};
  ...
  ret = mpfs_syscontroller_read_design_info(sys_serv_priv, designid,
                                            &designver, &designbacklevel);
  ...
  for (idx = 0; idx < 16; idx++)
          sprintf(&serialstring[2*idx], "%02x", device_serial_number[idx]);

  env_set("serial_number", serialstring);

device_serial_number[] is declared and zero-initialized but, in this hunk,
is never populated from the mailbox before being hex-encoded into
serial_number - it's not an output parameter of
mpfs_syscontroller_read_design_info() (which only fills designid/
designver/designbacklevel). Unless device_serial_number is filled earlier
in board_late_init() by a different call (e.g. mpfs_syscontroller_read_
sernum(), which exists per include/mpfs-mailbox.h) that isn't shown in this
diff context, env_set("serial_number", ...) will always report all-zero
bytes ("00000000000000000000000000000000"). Can you confirm
device_serial_number is actually populated earlier in the function (this
diff is a hunk inside a larger existing function, so it's possible this is
a non-issue if an earlier unchanged line already calls read_sernum()) -
if so no action needed, just want it confirmed since the new design_id/
design_ver output is otherwise clean and well laid out.

Also: CMD_DESIGN_INFO is now a real #define per Jamie Gibbons' v3 suggestion
- good, that addresses the earlier magic-number concern.
